      "id": "1084f837-a67a-4f2f-8fcb-b24dde4a0bd9/0",
      "statement": "For a complete non-Archimedean field K, K-affinoid spectra are compact Hausdorff with the initial topology of evaluations; affinoid algebras are noetherian with closed ideals, their spectra map surjectively to prime spectra, and reduced affinoid spectral norms are equivalent to their Banach norms. Rational domains with positive real radii are analytic domains and give neighbourhoods with strict defining inequalities inside prescribed ordinary opens. Good spaces have affinoid neighbourhood bases; good strict spaces over nontrivially valued K have strict affinoid neighbourhood bases. Ordinary opens are analytic domains, analytic sections form a sheaf, and O(M(A))=A. Affinoid scalar extensions and fibre products use completed tensors and scalar projections are surjective. Finite affinoid algebras carry their finite-module Banach topology. Analytic dimension is invariant under complete scalar extension and reduction, agrees with Krull dimension for strict affinoids, is preserved locally on analytic domains, and is the maximum of the dimensions of components through a point. Affinoid components are finite in number; irreducible analytic spaces are pure and proper closed analytic subsets have smaller dimension. Reduced good analytic spaces have finite normalization, and regularity, normality, and Serre conditions persist on analytic domains. Over a perfect field, a normal analytic curve is quasi-smooth; on good spaces, smoothness is quasi-smoothness together with absence of analytic boundary. CLD §§11.1–11.4 and GR Lemma 3.9.",
