      "id": "1084f837-a67a-4f2f-8fcb-b24dde4a0bd9/10",
      "statement": "For a separated pure one-dimensional analytic curve over a complete non-Archimedean field, GR subharmonicity is defined by every nontrivially valued complete analytic extension and every smooth connected strict curve probe, allowing an identically −∞ alternative. A finite continuous function is GR harmonic when both signs are GR subharmonic. Harmonic functions form an ordinary sheaf, harmonicity is equivalent before and after every complete analytic extension, and analytic curve maps preserve harmonicity, with converse for finite surjective maps. On smooth strict curves over nontrivially valued fields this harmonicity agrees with Thuillier harmonicity. On every analytic curve, GR subharmonicity equals classical psh defined using every analytic extension and every separated pure analytic curve probe; therefore two-sign GR harmonicity implies classical pluriharmonicity with those full quantifiers. GR Definitions 3.4, 3.13, 4.1, and 7.1, 3.4.2, 3.12, Proposition 3.14, and 4.1.2.",
