The full completion criteria are not established by this audit. I reconstructed the main T–C–B–Q dependency structure and found no counterexample to Theorem R or Theorem P. However, indispensable imported proof chains remain unresolved. Published statements, supplied transcriptions, and source citations do not substitute for the requested independent verification of their proofs.

The scope being audited is exactly the stated partial result: arbitrary complete nontrivially valued ground fields; actual good separated strict boundaryless pure curves, including singular and nonreduced structures; finite continuous functions; and ordinary CLD currents on every ordinary open. P concerns ground-field smooth strict curve probes. The original arbitrary-dimensional classification is expressly unclaimed and is not an additional completion requirement.

The supplied transport derivation addresses the principal potential gaps. Its finite separable argument uses actual tensors and Galois invariants, polarization into traces of product units, and the orbit polynomial with repeated roots and coefficient cancellations retained. The hull power sums have matching first derivatives across collision walls. The tangential mollification argument also treats moment segments contained in those walls. The total-variation estimate is made on calibrated carriers before signed pushforward. Fixed outer supports and the order of the two approximation limits prevent varying inner partitions from invalidating the estimate. These calculations do not merely establish uniform density of invariant functions.

The purely inseparable argument uses actual Frobenius powers, a homeomorphic scalar projection, and exact ordinary scalar-test descent. The mixed tower is embedded in the given finite extension. At completion, the rational-domain argument checks strict inequalities, equalities, vanishing denominators, and zero-seminorm coefficients using Banach approximation rather than reduction. Unit approximations give exact norms, and the compact partition produces an exact global finite-stage scalar-section identity. The separate local-purity argument avoids inferring purity from equality of total dimensions. These steps are conditional on the analytic foundations in premises 0–6, 7, 9, 18, and 19 being fully established.

The calibration distinctions are correct in the supplied applications: scalar projection has no field-degree factor; restriction of an F-analytic carrier to K multiplies its calibration by [F:K]; and component integration retains actual generic Artinian lengths. The source statements supporting these distinctions occur in [CLD](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.6277v2). No pullback of an arbitrary continuous current is used.

The algebraically closed recognition chain separates reduced components away from their rigid intersections, removes punctures using shrinking cutoffs with uniformly bounded Hessian total variation, and transports through normalization on the actual isomorphism locus before removing upstairs punctures. The zero-fibre cutoff weight is its Artinian length times the full residue-field degree. The local algebraic model must be an actual affinoid-domain embedding, and intrinsic absolute interiors are then needed to obtain ordinary model opens. Wanner’s comparison has the required algebraically closed field and smooth proper algebraic-curve scope; it is applied to both signs through inclusion of her tests in ordinary analytic CLD tests. The unresolved embedding-compatibility and Thuillier foundations remain essential here.

The algebraic-closure completion argument itself works for inseparable polynomials: bounded roots of sufficiently close monic approximants can be selected successively at distances tending to zero, giving a root in the completion. The descent argument also has the required quantifiers. An arbitrary analytic extension L/K is compared through a joint complete extension of L and the completed algebraic closure of K. Harmonic boundary majorants, scalar boundary transport, and scalar surjectivity are required for comparison descent. Recognition is then transported through all curve maps. This is function-theoretic descent, not an inference that numerical triviality alone establishes universal classical pluriharmonicity. The relevant statements and joint-field proof occur in [GR](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.13548v2).

For Q, the supplied positive-limit multiplication proof gives the necessary uniform coefficient-measure bounds. Its cutting argument imposes S1 on reduced component sources before using calibrated Poincare–Lelong, preserves the ambient equation by a doubly closed smooth wedge, and only then passes to the positive divisor-cycle limit. Successive cuts use constructed effective cycles, not an unidentified iterated quotient cycle. The local-image argument treats full Banach factorization through finite rigid constants, actual finite fibre algebras, all analytic source points, and ordinary interiors. Consequently its intended deduction of P is correctly distinguished from universal classical recognition in a higher-dimensional ambient space.

The indispensable unresolved checks are these. Premise 7’s BGR proof and premise 9’s original Berkovich proofs could not be retrieved in full: the PDFs exceeded the retrieval limit. Premise 11’s controlling Thuillier thesis returned an access denial. The supplied transcripts permit checking applications, but I did not independently authenticate and close those original proof chains. For premise 16, the Bosch–Lütkebohmert flattening source exposed only a subscription page; the formal-model lifting, flattening, extension, and finite-presentation dependencies used by GJR and GM therefore remain incompletely verified. Further nested proof checks in premises 19 and 22 also remain unsettled.

There is an additional substantive scope check for premises 17, 23, and 25. [KRZB Section 2.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1504.00694) assumes an algebraically closed ground field, and its slope formula concerns a smooth proper curve with a semistable model. Thus GJR’s reference ‘as in’ that theorem is not a direct application to arbitrary-field weighted strict affinoid models. The required localization and residue-degree-weighted slope reconstruction has not been fully completed here. This prevents certification of the entire GJR equivalence chain; it does not establish that the claimed equivalence is false.

Two imported proofs require explicit localization corrections. In GR’s finite-surjective descent proof, a curve fibre product can have isolated zero-dimensional components: pulling the normalization of xy=0 back along one branch gives a line together with an isolated point. Retaining its one-dimensional components fixes the curve hypothesis; their finite image covers the connected smooth probe because it covers the complement of the isolated-component images and is closed. In Wanner’s smoothing proof, the inverse image of an edge segment including a positive-genus skeletal vertex need not be an annulus. The smoothing chart should instead lie strictly inside the open edge around the transition region, where the differences glue to the original function on one side and zero on the other. These are local corrections, not counterexamples to R or P. A strictly positive approximation tolerance also avoids the case where Wanner’s stated error bound is zero.

The BGR strict Nullstellensatz citation should be 6.1.2/3 rather than 6.1.2/1. The draft’s GJR numbering corrections agree with the retrieved v3 statements. The suspected sign issue in Q’s symmetrization is harmless: CLD’s convention includes J(d''x)=-d'x, so the stated symmetrization is correct. The draft also correctly excludes the overbroad moment-dimension equality and the invalid inference from one regular element to S1.

No all-analytic-field ordinary-current ascent, harmonic approximant theorem, ordinary smoothness, or two-sign ordinary psh regularization is established or silently added. The ledger below records the load-bearing external claims separately from the supplied derivations. Passage indices are zero-based and match their corresponding claims. Short quotations locate retrieved statements; they do not certify every clause or its proof. No authenticated passage is supplied for premises 7, 9, or 11, and the remaining incomplete proof checks are explicitly identified above. These unresolved requirements mandate INCONCLUSIVE.

ALL PREMISES
0. For a complete non-Archimedean field K, K-affinoid spectra are compact Hausdorff with the initial topology of evaluations; affinoid algebras are noetherian with closed ideals, their spectra map surjectively to prime spectra, and reduced affinoid spectral norms are equivalent to their Banach norms. Rational domains with positive real radii are analytic domains and give neighbourhoods with strict defining inequalities inside prescribed ordinary opens. Good spaces have affinoid neighbourhood bases; good strict spaces over nontrivially valued K have strict affinoid neighbourhood bases. Ordinary opens are analytic domains, analytic sections form a sheaf, and O(M(A))=A. Affinoid scalar extensions and fibre products use completed tensors and scalar projections are surjective. Finite affinoid algebras carry their finite-module Banach topology. Analytic dimension is invariant under complete scalar extension and reduction, agrees with Krull dimension for strict affinoids, is preserved locally on analytic domains, and is the maximum of the dimensions of components through a point. Affinoid components are finite in number; irreducible analytic spaces are pure and proper closed analytic subsets have smaller dimension. Reduced good analytic spaces have finite normalization, and regularity, normality, and Serre conditions persist on analytic domains. Over a perfect field, a normal analytic curve is quasi-smooth; on good spaces, smoothness is quasi-smoothness together with absence of analytic boundary. CLD §§11.1–11.4 and GR Lemma 3.9.
1. For analytic morphisms X→Y→S, relative interiors compose by inclusion, with equality when Y→S is locally separated. Finite morphisms have no relative boundary. The relative interior of an analytic domain is its ordinary topological interior. For every complete analytic extension L/K, ∂(X_L/Y_L)⊆π⁻¹∂(X/Y), and π(∂X_L)=∂X. GR 2.11 and Lemma 2.12.
2. On a topologically separated analytic space, ordinary CLD forms admit ordinary-open moment descriptions by analytic units and Euclidean smooth superforms; scalar smooth functions locally have the form φ(t_f1,…,t_fm). These forms form an ordinary sheaf, and moment pullback commutes with wedge products, J, d′, and d″. Moment nullity is determined by restriction to compact moment images, and numerical equality determines equality of scalar sections. On a good Hausdorff analytic space, every compact subset inside an ordinary open admits an ordinary smooth compact cutoff equal to one nearby. A CLD (p,q)-form has support disjoint from every closed analytic subset of dimension less than max(p,q). CLD §§3.3–3.4, 6.2, and 15.1–15.2.
3. A pure n-dimensional topologically separated analytic space has a canonical positive calibrated integration theory, compatible with analytic-domain restriction. Its top-cell calibrations use actual finite-flat fibre-algebra degrees, including nilpotent lengths. Smooth top forms define signed Radon measures finite on compact moment domains; lower-dimensional cells carry no top measure. Integration over a pure closed analytic subspace equals integration over its associated cycle with actual generic Artinian multiplicities. Generic multiplicities are constant under domain restriction; at corresponding generic points of scalar extension, the upstairs multiplicity equals the original multiplicity times the multiplicity of the base-changed reduction. CLD 13.2.6–8, §§14.3–14.5, Theorem 15.7.1, and Proposition 16.6.3.
4. Let X be an analytic space of level n and L/K a complete analytic extension. Canonical calibrations satisfy π_*μ_L=μ. For a locally integrable tropically Borel (n,n) G-form ω, π_*ω_L,Rad=ω_Rad, with no field-degree factor. If F/K is finite and an F-analytic level-n space is viewed on the same carrier as K-analytic, its calibration is multiplied by [F:K]. For a morphism between level-n spaces finite outside a closed analytic subset of dimension less than n, the pushforward of the measure of a locally integrable pulled-back (p,n)-form, p=n−1 or n, equals its downstairs measure multiplied by the calibrated degree. CLD 14.3.6, Remark 14.4.3, and Propositions 15.3.1 and 15.4.3.
5. On locally differentially separated calibrated tropical spaces, positive CLD currents have coefficient Radon measures and admit locally bounded Borel multiplication. Integration currents are positive, and wedging with a strongly positive smooth form preserves positivity. Differentiation is weakly continuous and satisfies the smooth Leibniz rule; ordinary-open restriction and compact direct image commute with differentiation. Canonical integration on a boundaryless pure analytic space is d′- and d″-closed. Green’s formula and finite-domain inclusion-exclusion hold on compact calibrated domains with their induced boundary calibrations. CLD §§5.9–5.11, 7.2–7.3, and 7.7.
6. A stalkwise regular analytic function on a pure r-dimensional analytic space defines a pure codimension-one zero space with its effective associated divisor cycle. Let M be a compact pure r-dimensional analytic space satisfying S1, let T be a regular pure one-dimensional analytic space, and let f:M→T be pure of relative dimension r−1. For a unit moment b and a rigid t∈T, the calibrated top-dimensional fibre moment polyhedron is locally constant near t. If T=A¹ and t=0, the calibrated top-dimensional image of (b,f), for sufficiently negative log|f|, is the product of the actual calibrated zero-fibre polyhedron with the negative interval carrying standard primitive calibration. Source boundary is permitted. CLD 13.7.1 and Theorem 14.7.2 with target dimension one.
7. Over every complete nontrivially valued field K, every maximal ideal of a strict K-affinoid algebra has residue field finite over K. Consequently a zero-dimensional strict K-affinoid algebra is finite-dimensional Artinian over K and has finitely many rigid analytic points. BGR strict affinoid Nullstellensatz.
8. Over a complete algebraically closed non-Archimedean field, every connected normal one-dimensional strict affinoid space is isomorphic to an affinoid subspace of a nonsingular complete algebraic curve over the same field. Van der Put, Theorem 1.1.
9. For Hausdorff strict analytic spaces, the rigid-to-Berkovich correspondence is fully faithful, and a rigid affinoid domain corresponds to a strict Berkovich affinoid domain with the same analytic algebra. For a morphism of good analytic spaces, finiteness at a point is equivalent to that point being isolated in its analytic fibre and lying in the relative interior; equivalently, it admits arbitrarily small affinoid neighbourhoods inducing a finite map. Finite analytic maps to affinoids correspond to finite affinoid algebra maps with their finite-module Banach topologies. Berkovich 1993, §§1.6 and 3.1, and CLD 11.4.4–5.
10. Let K be algebraically closed, complete, and nontrivially valued; let C be a smooth proper algebraic K-curve; let W⊆Cᵃⁿ be ordinary open; and let v:W→R be continuous. If v has positive CLD Hessian current in Wanner’s Definition 3.16, then v is Thuillier-subharmonic. Wanner, arXiv:1801.04713v3, Theorem 4.7.
11. Over a complete nontrivially valued field K, every pure one-dimensional rig-smooth strict affinoid becomes the generic fibre of a simply semistable formal curve after a finite separable extension. Harmonic functions on a rig-smooth strict affinoid are uniquely determined by arbitrary real values on its Shilov boundary. On smooth strict boundaryless curves, Thuillier harmonicity and subharmonicity are local, harmonic functions satisfy harmonic comparison, and already harmonic functions remain harmonic after every complete analytic field extension and under every analytic morphism between smooth strict curves. Thuillier Theorem 2.3.8, Lemma 2.3.9, and Propositions 2.3.18–19.
12. For a separated pure one-dimensional analytic curve over a complete non-Archimedean field, GR subharmonicity is defined by every nontrivially valued complete analytic extension and every smooth connected strict curve probe, allowing an identically −∞ alternative. A finite continuous function is GR harmonic when both signs are GR subharmonic. Harmonic functions form an ordinary sheaf, harmonicity is equivalent before and after every complete analytic extension, and analytic curve maps preserve harmonicity, with converse for finite surjective maps. On smooth strict curves over nontrivially valued fields this harmonicity agrees with Thuillier harmonicity. On every analytic curve, GR subharmonicity equals classical psh defined using every analytic extension and every separated pure analytic curve probe; therefore two-sign GR harmonicity implies classical pluriharmonicity with those full quantifiers. GR Definitions 3.4, 3.13, 4.1, and 7.1, 3.4.2, 3.12, Proposition 3.14, and 4.1.2.
13. Let K be complete and nontrivially valued, X any strict separated pure one-dimensional K-analytic curve, and h:X→R continuous. Then h is GR harmonic if and only if h is G-locally real PL and every residue class L_h(x)∈Pic(red(X,x))_R is numerically trivial. Reduction, singularities, and nonreduced structures are permitted. GR Proposition 3.15.
14. For good strict analytic spaces and a divisible coefficient subgroup Λ⊆R, Λ-PL functions pull back under analytic morphisms and complete scalar extension; Λ-piecewise linearity is equivalent on a space and its reduction. For a finite flat surjective morphism, h is Λ-PL if and only if its pullback is Λ-PL. The same equivalence holds for scalar extension to the completion of an algebraic field extension. GJR v3 Lemma 5.4, Proposition 5.11, and Corollary 5.12.
15. On a good strict analytic space over a nontrivially valued field, a Λ-PL germ, where Λ=Z or Λ is divisible, has a well-defined residue class in Pic(red(X,x))_Λ, functorial under analytic morphisms, reduction, and scalar extension. It is semipositive at x exactly when its residue is nef, equivalently when it locally comes from a nef formal model; it is harmonic at x exactly when the residue is numerically trivial. For an already Λ-PL function, semipositivity and harmonicity pull back under analytic maps and descend globally under proper surjective maps, are equivalent under every complete analytic scalar extension, and are equivalent under reduction. The pointwise scalar converse additionally requires equality of the stated residue-field transcendence degrees. For separated finite-type schemes, nefness and numerical triviality are equivalent under field extension and reduction and descend under proper surjective maps. GJR §§6–7, especially Propositions 7.1 and 7.6.
16. Locally, a finite flat surjective morphism of good strict analytic spaces over a complete nontrivially valued field can be extended to admissible formal models, simultaneously dominating finitely many specified line-bundle models, and after admissible formal blowups can be made finite and flat on those models. The resulting finite flat modules are locally of finite presentation and hence locally free. Formal models admit common dominating blowups, and compact strict analytic domains can be realized as generic fibres of formal open subsets after admissible blowup. The formal-model lifting and flattening results invoked in GJR Proposition 5.11 and GM Proposition 2.7.
17. Let K be complete and nontrivially valued and X a strict rig-smooth K-analytic curve. A function h:X→R is GJR harmonic if and only if it is harmonic in Thuillier’s sense, including the semistable weighted-graph description after finite Galois extension. At an interior semistable vertex, the weighted sum of outgoing slopes of an integral model function equals the degree of its residue line bundle on the corresponding proper residual component. Real harmonic functions satisfy the same criterion by real linear extension. GJR Proposition 15.7 and its supporting slope comparison.
18. For every morphism f:Y→X of analytic spaces over a complete non-Archimedean field, y↦dim_y(f) is upper semicontinuous for the analytic Zariski topology; in particular, each locus {y:dim_y(f)≥m} is Zariski closed. Ducros, Variation de la dimension relative, Theorem 4.9.
19. Let K be a complete non-Archimedean field and Γ⊆R_{>0} a subgroup with Γ·|K×| nontrivial. If X is a compact Γ-strict K-analytic space of dimension d and f_1,…,f_m are analytic units, its norm moment image is a finite polytope with rational exponents and constants in the divisible hull of Γ·|K×|, of dimension at most d. Its boundary image is contained in such a polytope of dimension at most d−1. Ducros, Espaces de Berkovich, polytopes, squelettes et théorie des modèles, Theorem 3.2, with its erratum.
20. Let V be a paracompact strict analytic space over a complete nontrivially valued field K and L a line bundle on V. A formal metric on L restricted to a compact strict analytic domain extends to a formal metric on L; piecewise linear metrics are precisely formal metrics. If V is compact and F is the completion of an algebraic extension of K embedded in the completed algebraic closure, every piecewise linear or piecewise rational-linear metric on L_F descends to such a metric over a finite embedded subextension K′/K in F. GM Propositions 2.7, 2.10, and 2.18(b).
21. An analytic morphism is without relative boundary at a germ exactly when its induced morphism of germ reductions is proper. Scalar extension of germ reductions is described by the corrected graded extension functor E_ψ for the induced map of graded residue fields. Relative interiors compose with equality when the intervening target morphism is locally separated. For admissible formal schemes over K°, a formal morphism is proper, respectively locally proper, exactly when its generic-fibre analytic morphism is proper, respectively without boundary. Temkin, Local Properties I, Corollary 4.4, and Local Properties II, Theorem 5.2, Lemma 5.3, and Corollary 5.7.
22. For every complete analytic field extension L/K and every morphism g of K-analytic spaces, g is without relative boundary if and only if its actual scalar extension g_L is without relative boundary. Conrad–Temkin, Descent for non-Archimedean analytic spaces, Theorem 11.5.
23. Let K be algebraically closed, complete, and nontrivially valued; let C be a smooth proper geometrically connected K-curve; and let C_model be a split semistable K°-model whose skeleton is not a point. For a model line bundle L_model and nonzero meromorphic section s of its generic-fibre line bundle, F=−log||s|| restricted to the skeleton is tropical meromorphic and satisfies τ_*div(s)=div(F)+Σ_ζ deg(L_model|D_ζ)[ζ], where D_ζ is the residual component associated to ζ. KRZB Section 2.3 and Theorem 2.6.
24. Every separated pure one-dimensional analytic space over a complete non-Archimedean field is good and paracompact, including singular and nonreduced curves. Ducros, La structure des courbes analytiques, Proposition 3.3.7 and Theorem 4.5.10.
25. Over an algebraically closed complete nontrivially valued field, the outgoing slope of −log|g| at an interior type-two point of a separated quasi-smooth strict analytic curve, for a nonzero analytic germ g normalized to have norm one there, equals the order of its reduction at the residual point corresponding to the branch. On a generalized annulus, −log|g| for a meromorphic g is piecewise affine with integral slopes, and its change of slope equals the number of retracting poles minus zeros, counted with multiplicity. BPR Proposition 2.10 and Theorem 5.15(3), with the analytic-germ extension recorded in Cohen–Temkin–Trushin Remark 3.6.6.

