\Cref{thm:supplied-p061-singular-pluriharmonic} settles P06.1: classical
pluriharmonicity implies local real PL regularity throughout the original
scope, including arbitrary singular points. P06.2 retains the reverse
bridge from a current equation to the curve-test notion.

\begin{problem}\label{prob:frontier-pluriharmonic}
Let $K$ be complete and nontrivially valued and let $X$ be a good,
separated, strict, boundaryless $K$-analytic space. If a continuous $h$
satisfies $\dprimeddprime[h]=0$, must $h$ be classically pluriharmonic,
or harmonic under the hypotheses where a classical harmonic notion is
available, outside the positive cases recorded below?
\end{problem}

The residual problem has two logically separate gates.  One must first
control the zero-current equation after scalar extension, and one must then
regularize continuous positive-current functions into the CLD-psh
hierarchy.  The next proposition records that these two gates suffice and
that every counterexample must obstruct at least one of them.

