{"id": "n2", "summary": "Additional draft consequence: every ground-field smooth strict curve probe of a continuous ordinary zero-Hessian function on a good separated strict boundaryless pure ambient space is harmonic over arbitrary K. Closed-curve restriction and actual finite local images are reconstructed. This does not give universal higher-dimensional classical recognition.", "detailedSummary": "Using n1's arbitrary-field intrinsic recognition, the draft proves harmonicity of h∘f for every analytic K-map from a separated smooth strict curve into an ordinary open of a pure ambient space in the original P06.2 scope. It reconstructs intrinsic restriction to closed analytic curves through positive effective cuts with actual component lengths, continuous multiplication of positive weak limits, an S1 Poincare–Lelong proof, compact test lifting, and rigid puncture removal. The local-image proof gives either a rigid constant map or an actual finite map onto a reduced closed pure curve image, with analytic surjectivity and a finite normalization lift checked. Applying recognition directly to the image avoids assuming its normalization smooth over imperfect K. The resulting probe function is Thuillier/GR harmonic, classically pluriharmonic, G-locally real PL, and GJR harmonic. Probes into scalar-extended higher-dimensional spaces remain outside this conclusion.", "text": "Assuming the curve theorem proved in n1, the additional mathematical claim is this: over every complete nontrivially valued K, let X be good separated strict boundaryless and pure of dimension n≥1, U⊂X ordinary open, and h:U→R continuous finite with actual ordinary CLD D[h]=0. For every analytic K-map f:Y→U from a separated smooth strict K-analytic curve, h∘f is Thuillier harmonic and GR-harmonic, classically pluriharmonic on Y, G-locally real PL, and GJR harmonic. Smooth includes absence of absolute analytic boundary. This is Theorem P in P06.2-argument.md; it uses n1's result and no previous solver-note theorem.\n\nThe restriction argument is intrinsic. On the ordinary interior of a strict affinoid neighbourhood, decompose the ambient integration cycle into reduced pure components with their actual generic lengths. Reduced noetherian rings satisfy S1 by finite prime avoidance. The draft proves Poincare–Lelong on such S1 sources from CLD14.7.2 with target dimension one and Green's formula. Its compact calibrated product near f=0 retains the actual zero-fibre lengths; the inner face gives the divisor integral and side faces vanish by dimension. The unsupported assertion in CLD16.7.5 that one regular element forces an arbitrary ring to be S1 is excluded.\n\nFor a regular cutting function on every component of a positive r-dimensional integration cycle S, use smooth convex truncations w_m of log|f| and the positive doubly closed form α_m=Dw_m. The currents α_m∧S converge to the effective component divisor cycle S_f. Continuous multiplication preserves this positive weak convergence: for a fixed compact symmetric test, a positive smooth coefficient-matrix majorant bounds the coefficient measures; compact-uniform smooth approximation of the continuous multiplier then proves convergence. The smooth product rule gives D(hα_m∧S)=α_m∧D(hS)=0, so D(hS_f)=0.\n\nChoose cutting functions in the closed curve's ideal while avoiding every current component prime. A component of dimension greater than one cannot lie in the curve, so that ideal is not contained in any such prime. Finite avoidance over the infinite field supplies each function. After n−1 proper cuts, the resulting finite effective curve cycle contains every reduced component of the prescribed closed curve. Distinct resulting curve components intersect only in a finite rigid set. On an ordinary open excluding the other components, compact test lifting through its closed immersion separates its intrinsic equation with its actual positive weight. Rigid puncture removal, reconstructed in the main file, extends this equation over the intersections. Finally recombine the prescribed curve's own generic lengths. Thus D[h|C]=0 intrinsically for every closed pure analytic curve C, including curves contained in the ambient singular locus and nonreduced structures. No arbitrary quotient is declared S1 and no global multiplicity is divided out.\n\nFor the probe map, choose connected normal strict affinoid source and strict affinoid target neighbourhoods. Their bounded algebra map has two branches. If all pulled-back Tate coordinates are algebraic over K, they generate a finite field E inside the reduced source algebra. That field is complete and closed in the source Banach algebra; limits of polynomial partial sums show the entire bounded Tate map factors through E. The map is therefore rigid constant locally, including inseparable E/K.\n\nOtherwise a pulled-back coordinate b is transcendental over K. At any rigid source point a, its value is algebraic over K. Its minimal polynomial P_a makes P_a(b) a nonzero nonunit in a one-dimensional domain. The quotient by it is a zero-dimensional strict affinoid and finite over K. The actual fibre over f(a), with its completed residue field, is a closed subspace of the corresponding scalar-extended finite zero algebra, hence has dimension zero. CLD11.4.5's Zariski upper semicontinuity and the strict Nullstellensatz then show that the positive-relative-dimension locus is empty: a nonempty closed locus would have a rigid point. The separated-target boundary equality makes the original map relatively boundaryless, so the finite-at-a-point criterion supplies actual analytic neighbourhoods on which it is finite. It does not require compactness of the original entire fibre.\n\nShrink the target to a strict affinoid neighbourhood and take the connected source component containing the chosen point. Its algebra B is normal and a domain. For A the target algebra, the prime kernel gives A_0=A/ker(A→B), a reduced strict affinoid domain algebra; B is finite integral over A_0 and both have dimension one. The closed analytic image C=M(A_0) is therefore a pure curve. Analytic surjectivity is checked at every image point c: lying-over supplies a nonzero finite fibre algebra over κ(ker c), extending to H(c) keeps it nonzero, and a finite residue field with its extending norm gives an actual analytic fibre point. The finite integral closure of A_0 embeds boundedly into B by normality, giving the finite normalization lift. No separability or finite-flatness assertion is used.\n\nPass now to the actual ordinary target interior V^o containing f(y). C^o=C∩V^o is closed in a good separated strict boundaryless pure ambient open and itself a good separated strict boundaryless pure curve. The reconstructed restriction lemma gives D[h|C^o]=0. Apply n1 directly over this same possibly imperfect field to recognize h|C^o. GR3.14(7) pulls back this already harmonic function to the ordinary source neighbourhood. The normalization of C^o is not asserted smooth over K and is unnecessary for this recognition step. Locality covers every analytic source point. GR3.4.2 and 3.12 identify the conclusion with Thuillier harmonicity on the smooth strict source; GR4.1.2 and 3.15 supply its classical and real-PL/GJR conclusions.\n\nThe complete proof, source statements and primary bodies are in P06.2-argument.md, with standalone P06.2-partial.tex and twice-compiled P06.2-partial.pdf. Rerun python3 prepare.py and python3 build-and-check.py in /workspace/research/campaigns/round-13/P06.2-arbitrary-field-curve-recognition-continuation/artifacts/codex-mNUPp2. The shared build evidence reports 128 pages and clean final TeX diagnostics; those checks do not verify the theorem.\n\nThis result is strictly ground-field recognition of probe functions. It supplies no current equation on X_L for arbitrary transcendental L/K and therefore does not establish classical pluriharmonicity of the original higher-dimensional h with all extension probes. It adds no result for non-strict ground-field source curves, ordinary smooth harmonic approximants, or two-sign ordinary positive-current regularization. Original arbitrary-dimensional P06.2 remains open and candidate remains false.\n\nArtifacts: /workspace/research/campaigns/round-13/P06.2-arbitrary-field-curve-recognition-continuation/artifacts/codex-mNUPp2", "support": ["n1"]}
