{"id": "n1", "summary": "Reconstructed draft proves intrinsic ordinary-CLD zero-Hessian recognition on every good separated strict boundaryless pure analytic curve over every complete nontrivially valued field, including singular and nonreduced curves. It gives universal classical pluriharmonicity, G-local real PL, and GJR harmonicity. Original arbitrary-dimensional P06.2 remains open.", "detailedSummary": "The draft removes the algebraically closed ground-field restriction without imposing scalar stability. It reconstructs completed-algebraic current transport using finite separable differentiated scalar-test approximation, finite Frobenius transport, mixed finite towers, exact finite-stage descent of compact scalar tests, and local purity. After passage to the completed algebraic closure, component-weight separation, rigid puncture removal, normalization transport, actual ordinary local algebraic models, and Wanner's correctly scoped comparison recognize the function. GR harmonicity then descends with its full analytic-extension and curve-map quantifiers. Actual nilpotent integration multiplicities are retained. The result does not establish arbitrary-dimensional recognition, ordinary smooth harmonic approximation, two-sign ordinary psh regularization, or the optional ordinary-current ascent under every analytic extension. The complete argument and primary bodies are embedded in the authoritative manuscript. This remains an author-self-audited, unverified draft.", "text": "The mathematical claim is the following curve-scope theorem. Let K be any complete nontrivially valued field, X any good separated strict boundaryless K-analytic space pure of dimension one, U any ordinary open in X, and h:U→R continuous and finite. If the actual ordinary CLD current D[h]=d'd''[h] vanishes, then h is GR-harmonic and classically pluriharmonic, G-locally real PL, and GJR harmonic. No perfectness, reducedness, normality, global properness, algebraicity, or input scalar-stability hypothesis is imposed. Classical pluriharmonicity has the full quantifiers: after every complete analytic extension L/K and every analytic curve map Y→U_L, both pulled-back signs are GR-subharmonic.\n\nThe authoritative complete draft is P06.2-argument.md in the assigned workspace. Its standalone typesetting is P06.2-partial.tex and its twice-compiled PDF is P06.2-partial.pdf. status.json has exactly id, status, exact_result, proof_file, concrete_gaps and records status=partial. self-audit.md, research-notes.md, source-manifest.json, and evidence/ contain the dependency audit, failed routes, hashes, environment, commands, and outputs. Rerun from the workspace with python3 prepare.py followed by python3 build-and-check.py. The workspace is /workspace/research/campaigns/round-13/P06.2-arbitrary-field-curve-recognition-continuation/artifacts/codex-mNUPp2.\n\nThe first proof obligation is ordinary-current transport. On a pure curve, vanishing means ∫hDψ=0 for every compact ordinary smooth scalar test ψ; the unknown Hessian is not presumed to be a measure. For a finite Galois scalar extension, average a test over the finite group using real coefficients. Full-fibre shrinking gives unit-moment descriptions near every downstairs point. Approximate their Euclidean coefficients through order two by polynomials. Scalar polarization expresses every averaged mixed polynomial as a finite combination of traces of powers of logarithms of product units. The orbit polynomial has downstairs coefficients. Its Newton hull expresses these traces as piecewise polynomial functions of the nonzero coefficient valuations; coefficients vanishing at the selected point are omitted only after a strict inequality proves they cannot enter the lower hull.\n\nThose Newton-hull functions are C1: when collinear segments merge, their gradients telescope. Their locally bounded chamber Hessians have no singular face contribution. Mollification converges in the tangent Hessian on every calibrated curve segment, including segments wholly contained in collision walls. On an enlarged moment chart, the positive top form Σd't_i∧d''t_i dominates the differentiated error on the actual supporting skeleton. Bounded convergence yields carrier total-variation convergence before any signed pushforward. Inner cutoffs may depend on polynomial degree, but every approximate support stays inside one fixed outer compact set. Thus pairing the error with any continuous finite h tends to zero. CLD14.3.6 and 15.3.1 transport pulled-back smooth top measures with no scalar degree factor. The separate same-carrier field-restriction calibration has the full finite degree; it is used only to justify the group action on the upstairs pairing. This proves finite separable ascent, with normal-closure descent handling non-Galois stages.\n\nFor a finite purely inseparable extension F/K, choose q=p^e with F^q⊂K. In the actual algebra A⊗K F every element has its qth power in A. Scalar projection is a homeomorphism, and each upstairs unit logarithm is q^-1 times a downstairs unit logarithm. Consequently compact scalar tests descend exactly. Every finite algebraic extension is treated through its actual embedded separable/purely inseparable tower, including all nilpotents.\n\nFor L equal to the completion of an algebraic extension M/K, completed-tensor density approximates finitely many analytic functions by functions over a finite embedded stage. A positive lower bound for the weighted maximum of a rational system's denominator and numerators makes sufficiently small perturbations preserve both the full weak conjunction and its strict locus exactly. On a compact rational domain, approximation below each unit's positive minimum preserves its norm exactly. The same smooth coefficient therefore defines a finite-stage scalar section whose pullback is the original section. A common finite stage and an explicit finite compact partition produce exact global descent of every compact ordinary scalar test. Compact inverse images and local purity are checked separately. The calibrated smooth top-measure identity then proves completed-algebraic ascent and descent of the actual current equation.\n\nThe second obligation is recognition, separate from transport. Equip an algebraic closure of K with its extending valuation and complete it to C. The draft proves C algebraically closed: approximate any monic polynomial by monic polynomials over the algebraic closure with rapidly decreasing coefficient errors; recursively choose roots whose successive distances tend to zero using the factored evaluation bound. Their bounded Cauchy sequence converges to a root. C is complete and nontrivially valued because it contains K isometrically. The transport theorem therefore gives D[h_C]=0 on the actual pure curve U_C.\n\nFor algebraically closed recognition, CLD16.6.3 decomposes integration into reduced components with their original generic Artinian lengths. Closed-embedding compact tests lift by analytic-unit lifting and compact cutoffs. Away from the finite rigid intersections, the current equation separates each component with its actual positive weight. Rigid scalar test germs are locally constant: the minimal polynomial of a unit's value forces its norm to remain constant on a sufficiently small neighbourhood. At a rigid point a, a regular analytic function with singleton zero fibre gives shrinking smooth cutoffs χ_m with uniformly bounded variation of Dχ_m and zero integral. CLD14.7.2 applies with target dimension one and an already S1 source; the bound contains the actual length times the full residue-field degree. A current supported at a is therefore cψ(a) on scalar tests, and continuity gives |c|≤M sup_supportχ_m|h-h(a)|→0. This removes rigid punctures without assuming D[h] is a measure.\n\nNormalization is an actual isomorphism outside a locally finite rigid set: a common regular denominator clears its finite integral-closure generators. Transport on that isomorphism locus followed by puncture removal proves the upstairs equation. Over the algebraically closed field the normalization is smooth. Van der Put's Theorem1.1 applies only to a connected normal one-dimensional strict affinoid over that algebraically closed complete field. Thuillier's explicit Berkovich-domain formulation and the separated boundaryless interior equality identify actual ordinary neighbourhoods with ordinary opens of smooth proper algebraic curves. Wanner, arXiv:1801.04713v3, Theorem4.7 applies exactly there: a continuous current-psh function is Thuillier-subharmonic. Her algebraic moment tests are ordinary analytic CLD tests with the same canonical integration. The zero equation supplies positivity for both signs. Dirichlet comparison recognizes harmonicity. GR3.14(7) descends already recognized harmonicity through normalization and the reduction immersion.\n\nFull field quantifiers are checked after recognition. For any analytic extension L/K, embed the algebraic closure of K into an algebraic closure of L and complete the latter to a common complete field F containing C and L isometrically. F/C is not asserted algebraic. GR3.5(7), whose full proof is included, descends subharmonicity using this joint field and compact harmonic-majorant comparison on smooth strict probes. GR3.14(6), 4.1.2, 4.2(5), and 7.2(6) then give the full recognized-function descent. GR3.15 applies over the original nontrivially valued K to every strict analytic curve and yields real PL plus numerical triviality of all residual classes, precisely GJR harmonicity. The historical GJR references in GR3.15 are explicitly matched to the cached Lemma5.4(3) and Corollary5.12 statements and proof bodies. No weak-current substitution or ordinary smooth harmonic approximation is made.\n\nThe evidence is artifact evidence, not mathematical verification. The final build ran two successful XeLaTeX passes and produced 128 pages with zero overfull boxes, missing-character warnings, or undefined-reference warnings. All 29 corrected primary passages bind exactly to their newline-only ranges; 31 additional primary ranges are included. A bounded finite rational 2-adic diagnostic passed 1720 weak/strict rational-conjunction comparisons. The first source-binding attempt failed because two extractions of the same CLD PDF had different newline offsets; the repaired program distinguishes them and asserts equal PDF hashes. The first TeX parsing failure and its exact files are preserved. The authoritative manuscript SHA-256 is 3aa30e90ed6ab6c645dcb1574c0e54ad9b5e436232c9cfa562089f961b1dbd09.\n\nThis is a new arbitrary-field curve-scope draft theorem, not a complete resolution of original P06.2. Complementary ordinary tests of positive bidegree and current ascent on higher-dimensional ambient spaces remain unproved. Two-sign ordinary psh regularization is not constructed. The optional implication from recognized harmonicity to zero ordinary analytic CLD current after every analytic extension is not reconstructed and is omitted. P05.2 is not addressed. No predecessor theorem, note, review, or hash is used as a mathematical premise; all needed solver-draft arguments are supplied anew in the main file.\n\nArtifacts: /workspace/research/campaigns/round-13/P06.2-arbitrary-field-curve-recognition-continuation/artifacts/codex-mNUPp2", "support": []}
