      "id": "n2",
      "summary": "Proved that an admissible relative Tate presentation with strictly contracted generator spectral radii is finite, including its Banach topology. This reconstructs the final finiteness step in Berkovich’s local argument.",
      "detailedSummary": "A geometric tail estimate produces finitely many module generators and bounded coefficient lifts. Applied after compact shrinking, it replaces the nested finiteness invocation in Berkovich 1993 Proposition 3.1.4. Goodness, relative interior, admissibility and actual affinoid base change remain explicit hypotheses. No recognition or higher-dimensional probe theorem follows from this repair alone.",
      "text": "Let A and B be complete non-Archimedean Banach K-algebras with ultrametric submultiplicative norms. Suppose q:A⟨r_1^{-1}T_1,…,r_m^{-1}T_m⟩→B is an admissible bounded surjection and b_i=q(T_i) has spectral radius ρ(b_i)<r_i. Then B is a finite Banach A-algebra, with the given norm equivalent to its finite-module quotient norm. Reducedness, perfectness and discreteness are unnecessary.\n\nAdmissibility gives C>0 such that every b has a lift F with ||F||≤C||b||. Choose ρ(b_i)<s_i<r_i. From the power spectral-radius formula, choose N_i with ||b_i^{N_i}||<s_i^{N_i}. Splitting exponents into multiples of N_i and remainders gives constants C_i with ||b_i^j||≤C_i s_i^j for all j. Let t=max(s_i/r_i)<1, C_0=∏C_i, and let D bound A→B. The image of the terms of F of total degree at least N has norm at most D C_0 t^N||F||. Choose N so η=D C_0 C t^N<1.\n\nLift b, keep only monomials of degree below N, and repeat on the remainder. Successive remainders have norms at most η^j||b||. For each of the finitely many retained monomials, the successive coefficients have norms at most Cη^j||b||r^{-ν}, so their sums converge in A. Consequently b is an A-linear combination of b^ν, |ν|<N. These coefficient lifts satisfy max ||a_ν||r^ν≤C||b||. The finite sum map is bounded in the other direction, proving equivalence of the finite-module quotient norm and the original norm. For m=0 the assertion is immediate.\n\nThe local source application is precise. Berkovich 1993 Proposition 3.1.4, for good source and target, characterizes finiteness at y by isolation of y in its fibre and membership in the relative interior. Its proof provides an admissible relative Tate presentation with |b_i(y)|<r_i. Shrink the source affinoid so its fibre over x contains only y. The locus where some |b_i|≥r_i is compact and its image misses x. A target affinoid neighbourhood U′ avoiding that image has affinoid inverse image V′, with the base-changed admissible presentation. Compactness gives sup_{V′}|b_i|<r_i. CLD 11.1.1 identifies this supremum with the spectral radius, so the proved contraction lemma supplies the finite Banach algebra. This retains nilpotents. Berkovich Lemma 3.1.5 and the stated relative-interior composition law then provide the ordinary-neighbourhood finite maps.\n\nThe full relevant Berkovich bodies are embedded in P06.2-argument.md. Cached PDF physical pages 52/53, printed 55/56, were rendered and viewed, including the original final reference to Berkovich 1990, 2.5.13. The contraction proof replaces that invocation in this exact presentation situation. The separate affinoid-domain application to van der Put is recorded using Berkovich 1.6.1 and 1.6.2(i): rigid affinoid domains correspond to actual Berkovich domains, not automatically ordinary opens. Ordinary interiors require the separate boundary-composition check.\n\nArtifacts and rerun commands are P06.2-argument.md, prepare.py and run-evidence.py in the fresh workspace. Run python3 prepare.py, or python3 run-evidence.py to reproduce renderings and captured command outputs. These executions do not verify the mathematics. Optional P0 still needs intrinsic current restriction and recognition; neither is inferred here.",
