      "summary": "Repaired the fibre-product dimension error: the reduced union of one-dimensional components of a finite surjective space over a strict pure curve still maps finitely and surjectively onto the curve.",
      "detailedSummary": "The proof retains legitimate pure curve probes while allowing isolated zero-dimensional fibre-product components. Their finite images do not obstruct surjectivity of the one-dimensional carrier. The normalization of xy=0 pulled back to a branch is explicitly a line plus an isolated point. This repairs a carrier step in GR descent, not the remaining harmonic-comparison theorem.",
      "text": "Let K be complete and nontrivially valued, Z good, separated, strict and pure one-dimensional, and p:W→Z finite and surjective. Let W^(1) be the reduced closed subspace supported on the union of the one-dimensional irreducible components of W. Then W^(1) is pure one-dimensional and W^(1)→Z is finite and surjective. This claim concerns carriers; it does not discard multiplicities in a current calculation.\n\nWork over a strict affinoid neighbourhood V⊂Z. The inverse image W_V is affinoid with finitely many components. Each component is finite over its closed image, so its dimension is at most one. This uses strict Krull dimension and finite integral dimension preservation, not flatness. Its zero-dimensional components have finite rigid carriers by n1. The union of its one-dimensional components with reduced structure is a closed affinoid, defined by the intersection of their prime ideals, hence finite over V. These subspaces agree under domain restriction because local analytic dimension on components is preserved; thus they glue to W^(1).\n\nLet F⊂V be the finite image of the zero-dimensional components. Surjectivity of W_V→V shows that the image of W^(1)_V contains V\\F. That image is closed: its source is compact and V is Hausdorff. The complement of F is dense. Indeed a finite set containing a nonempty open in a Hausdorff space with no isolated points would contain an open singleton.\n\nThe required absence of isolated points is checked with strictness. An isolated point in a good strict curve over nontrivial K has a strict affinoid neighbourhood whose analytic spectrum is a singleton. Surjectivity of that spectrum onto its prime spectrum forces Krull dimension zero, contradicting local dimension one. CLD 11.7.7 supplies the strict neighbourhood, and 11.1.2 and 11.2.3 supply exactly the spectrum and dimension statements. The same argument applies to V. It does not incorrectly assign dimension zero to every singleton non-strict affinoid carrier. Closedness and density now show that W^(1)_V maps onto V. Neighbourhoods cover Z, proving the result.\n\nThe nodal example is exact. For A=K⟨x,y⟩/(xy), normalization has algebra B=K⟨u⟩×K⟨v⟩, with branches (x,y)=(u,0) and (0,v). Pull back to D=K⟨t⟩ by x↦t,y↦0. The completed tensor factors are K⟨u,t⟩/(u−t)≅D and K⟨v,t⟩/(t,v)≅K. Thus B⊗̂_A D≅D×K. The full fibre product is not pure one-dimensional. Its one-dimensional carrier is the line, whose image covers the probe.\n\nIn GR Proposition 3.5(8), one must apply the curve-test definition to W^(1), not to the full fibre product W. Its finite images cover the connected smooth probe by the proof above. The subsequent normalization and harmonic-comparison steps remain separate obligations. This is not a counterexample to P06.2 or a completed GR descent proof.\n\nThe proof, exact standard foundation statements and source bodies appear in section D and Appendix S of P06.2-argument.md. Rebuild in the fresh workspace with python3 prepare.py.",
