      "detailedSummary": "The full prior report, 26 premises and 28 passages were inspected. A newly explicit source-scope gap is that Thuillier’s arbitrary-field finite-separable algebraization step cites van der Put’s paper, which assumes algebraic closedness throughout. Stable reduction, model realization and harmonic pullback remain separate dependencies even after GJR conclusions are omitted. All fresh artifacts are reproducible; 23 LF-only source ranges and the exact five-key status passed structural checks. These checks are not mathematical verification.",
      "text": "No unconditional intrinsic curve-recognition theorem is established in this submission. R0 retains its exact requested scope: every complete nontrivially valued K, every good separated strict boundaryless pure analytic curve, including singular and nonreduced structures, every ordinary open, finite continuous h, actual ordinary CLD zero Hessian, and all analytic-extension/all-curve-probe classical quantifiers. No perfectness, scalar-stability or algebraicity assumption is substituted. Original arbitrary-dimensional P06.2 remains open; candidate=false.\n\nThe full INCONCLUSIVE review was read, including all 26 premises and 28 passages. Canonical predecessor notes were obtained from the predecessor campaign run.json, not an alternate local notes file. Their claims and PASS reports are not theorem premises. The reconstructed repairs are n1’s strict residue finiteness, n2’s contracted-presentation finiteness, n3’s legitimate pure fibre-product carrier, and n4’s local annular smoothing.\n\nThe principal unresolved source application is now explicit. Thuillier Theorem 2.3.8 states semistable-model existence for every pure one-dimensional rig-smooth strict affinoid after a finite separable extension. Its proof first algebraizes after a finite separable extension, citing bibliography [31], theorem 1. The cached van der Put paper explicitly assumes algebraically closed k throughout. Its Theorem 1.1 covers the algebraically closed embedding application, but that statement alone does not justify the arbitrary-field finite-separable algebraization step. An additional descent/algebraization argument is required and has not been supplied here. This diagnoses a missing application argument, not a false theorem.\n\nThe same thesis proof then invokes bibliography [9], Bosch–Lütkebohmert, Stable reduction and uniformization of abelian varieties I, Math. Ann. 270 (1985), 349–379; it further needs boundary realization through Lemma 2.2.22 and realization as the generic f